  description="What the tidal coefficient means, how it is worked out and how to read it.">
  <article class="article">
    <h1>The tidal coefficient</h1>
    <p class="lede">One number, from about 20 to 120, for how strong the tide is on a given day.</p>
    <h2>Where it comes from</h2>
    <p>The coefficient is a French convention, used on the Atlantic and Channel coasts of France and widely by
      sailors, fishers and shell gatherers. It compares the day's tidal range with an average spring tide, scaled so
      that an average spring tide is about 95, an average neap tide about 45, and the largest tides of the
      19-year lunar cycle reach about 120.</p>
    <h2>How we work it out</h2>
    <p>The range of the tide follows the positions of the Moon and Sun: largest when they line up (new and full
      moon), and larger still when the Moon is close to the Earth or both are near the equator. We compute the
      coefficient from the Moon's and Sun's positions each day (JPL DE421 ephemeris). It is the same number for
      every station on a given day: it describes the astronomical drive, not the local range. Our values come close
      to the official French ones but are not identical.</p>
    <h2>Reading it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Below 45, low</strong>: neap tides, small range, gentle currents.</li>
      <li><strong>45 to 69, average</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>70 to 89, high</strong>: spring tides, strong currents in channels.</li>
      <li><strong>90 and above, very high</strong>: big springs, very low low waters, the highest high waters.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The coefficient is printed for every day in each station's month table, next to the moon phase it follows.</p>
  </article>
